<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/intermediate/what-a-business-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A business is <strong>a machine for creating and capturing value</strong> — and the two
    halves are separate skills. Many firms create value they never capture (inventions without
    patents, networks without monetisation); others capture value they never created (rents,
    tricks) until customers leave. The business model is the designed answer to both halves at
    once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="create, capture, hypothesise, measure" widget="equationStep" config={whatCfg} />

  <h2>Forms and their trade-offs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sole trader → partnership → company:</strong> liability protection, taxation, and control shift at each step. The corporate form (limited liability, 1600s VOC origin) is arguably history's most consequential legal invention.</li>
    <li><strong>Revenue models shape behaviour:</strong> subscription (retention obsession), transaction (volume), advertising (attention), freemium (conversion). Choose the model, inherit the incentives.</li>
    <li><strong>Stakeholders vs shareholders:</strong> who is the firm for? Friedman's "the social responsibility of business is profit" (1970) vs stakeholder capitalism — the argument is really about time horizon and measurement.</li>
    <li><strong>Every model is a bet on something staying true:</strong> tastes, technology, regulation. The strategy lesson asks which bets are defensible.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Osterwalder & Pigneur, Business Model Generation; Drucker, The Practice of Management; Friedman (1970) NYT essay.</p>
</TopicPage>
